# Rundfrage - local configuration
#
# None of this is required to start the system: compose.yaml carries the same
# values as inline defaults, so `docker compose up` works on a fresh clone with
# no .env present (FR-001, SC-002, research.md R-11).
#
# Copy to .env only when you want to override something. .env is git-ignored.

# --- Storage --------------------------------------------------------------
# One file in a mounted directory. There is no host, no port and no credential:
# reaching the storage means having the path (003 FR-002, FR-007).
DATA_DIR=/data

# Be clear about what that means: whoever can read this directory can read every
# poll and every answer in it, without a password (003 FR-007b). The application
# creates the file for its own account only and cannot do more than that.
#
# It is deliberately NOT encrypted (003 FR-007c) - a key kept beside the data in
# this same file would stop nobody who can reach the data, while adding a way to
# lose everything by losing a key. Protecting the directory is the host's job.
#
# Back it up through the admin area, not with `cp`: a hand copy taken while the
# system runs can be unusable (003 FR-003b).

# --- Application ----------------------------------------------------------
# Serilog minimum level. Change without rebuilding (FR-025).
# Verbose | Debug | Information | Warning | Error | Fatal
LOG_LEVEL=Information

# Host port the application is published on.
APP_PORT=8080

# Answers accepted per hour from one source (FR-027a). Leave unset in anything resembling
# production; end-to-end runs raise it because they submit far more than ten from one machine.
# SUBMISSION_LIMIT_PER_HOUR=1000

# --- Admin account (REQUIRED, no defaults) --------------------------------
# Exactly one operator account, configured here (FR-045). There is no
# registration and no password-change screen.
#
# The password itself must never appear in configuration (FR-045a). Generate a
# hash and put that here instead:
#
#   docker compose run --rm app dotnet Rundfrage.Api.dll --hash-password
#
# Unlike everything above, these have no working defaults: the application
# refuses to start without them. An admin area on a guessable built-in
# password would be worse than no protection at all.
ADMIN_USER=
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=
